  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 321 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nScattered showers have been blossoming across western Minnesota\nsince yesterday evening. A moisture gradient and a modest LLJ \nseem to be the main culprits. These showers may expand eastward\nmore during the next several hours before weakening by mid\nmorning. PoPs may need to be increased and expanded east if\ncurrent radar trends continue. A stronger mid level disturbance\nis passing across northern IA, which has allowed more \nwidespread and steadier showers south of the border. That wave\nis sliding east southeast and those showers will remain to the \nsouth. Later in the day and tonight, a short wave will pass\nthrough ND and northern MN. Storms may fire late this afternoon\nacross eastern ND and propagate east this evening. Steeper \nlapse rates will spread east to the Upper Midwest and a modest \nLLJ may allow the storms to continue into parts of central MN \nby late evening before weakening. \n\nA large and intense ridge will remain centered over the Four\nCorners to southern high Plains region through at least next\nweek. This lends lower confidence to the forecast locally\ncompared to when it was centered overhead last week for several\nreasons:\n\n1.) Several weak disturbances will traverse the periphery of \nthe ridge which may spark chances for thunderstorms at any time.\nSuch disturbances are very difficult to predict more than a \ncouple days in advance.\n\n2.) Elevated wildfire smoke in the west northwest flow aloft \ncould somewhat limit temperatures from reaching their max \npotential across the northern half of MN and WI. \n\n3.) Any stronger shortwave could beat back the ridge for a day \nor so. \n\nConfidence is high enough for heat and humidity to reach\ndangerous levels across southern MN and the metro Saturday and \nSunday such that an Extreme Heat Watch has been issued for \nthose areas. With temperatures well into the 90s and dew points\nin the mid to potentially upper 70s, heat indices should range\nfrom 100 to 108. Farther north, vertically integrated smoke \nmodeling shows higher concentrations across northern MN into \nWI, coupled with dew points remaining in the upper 60s. This\nshould keep the most intense heat at bay, although it is likely\nHeat Advisories will be needed.\n\nThere is a conditional risk of thunderstorm development Saturday\nnight. A strengthening LLJ and very steep lapse rates could lead\nto this development. However, rising heights and 700 mb temps\nlikely greater than +12C should limit coverage. It does not \nappear anything will develop on a scale that would negatively \nimpact temperatures for Sunday. \n\nBy Sunday night a cold front will approach from the northwest.\nAn unstable airmass with sufficient shear would be supportive of\nthunderstorms. However, the lack of a strong shortwave and a cap\nlikely remaining could again limit coverage. If storms do\ndevelop, there is a risk of them becoming severe.\n\nBehind the front Monday, winds will turn northwest and drier air\nwill work in during the afternoon. If this dry air advection\nslows, then another day of extreme heat is possible. Confidence\nis not high enough to extend the watch through Monday at this\ntime. The rest of next week looks seasonably hot, but less humid\nthan this weekend. We will be tallying another long string of\n90s to finish out the month. It's possible more than half this\nmonth will have highs in the 90s, getting us into the top 10\nmost number of days of 90+ for July.",
